\title{Simulation Style Guide}
\author{Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

This guide covers \texttt{.sim} in simulation projects.
Agents follow \texttt{.setup/ai/style/simulation.md},
which follows this guide and is changed together with it.
Existing simulations follow it when next reused.

\section{Layout}

\begin{itemize}
	\item \texttt{README.md}: how to set up, run, and summarize.
	It also marks the project as a simulation.
	\item \texttt{sim-args.R}: conditions, replications,
	the failure classes, and the attempts allowed.
	\item \texttt{sim.R} and \texttt{sim.sh}: one block of cells,
	and the SLURM job array.
	\item \texttt{sim-compress.R} and \texttt{sim-compress.sh}: collect and compress results.
	\item \texttt{sum-*.R} and \texttt{sum-*.sh}: summaries for the paper.
	\item \texttt{monitor.sh}: progress, and the cells to check.
\end{itemize}

The functions that do the work live in \texttt{R}, documented and tested;
scripts only call them.
Set the thread counts of OpenMP, MKL, and OpenBLAS to one:
the job array is the only parallel layer.

\section{Seeds}

A cell is one \texttt{taskid} and \texttt{repid}.
Its seed comes from those two and the attempt number,
so any cell can be rerun alone:

\begin{verbatim}
seed <- ((attempt - 1L) * tasks + (taskid - 1L)) * reps + repid
\end{verbatim}

No two cells or attempts share a seed
while \texttt{repid} is at most \texttt{reps} and \texttt{taskid} is at most \texttt{tasks}.
Keep the product of attempts, tasks, and replications below the largest integer in R.

\section{Failures}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Each project lists the failure classes that allow a new seed in \texttt{sim-args.R}.
	\item Anything else is a result, or an error to fix: it never gets a new seed.
	\item A listed failure moves the cell to the next attempt,
	up to the maximum in \texttt{sim-args.R}.
	\item A crash (a segfault, or an out-of-memory kill) counts as a failed attempt
	of the cell that was running.
	\item Never set a seed by hand for one cell.
	\item The cell's output records \texttt{taskid}, \texttt{repid}, \texttt{attempt}, the seed,
	and, for each failed attempt, its seed and failure class.
	It does not keep a failed attempt's results.
	\item The summary reports the reseeded cells per condition and the failure rate,
	with the results: keeping only successful attempts conditions on convergence.
\end{itemize}

\section{Output}

\begin{itemize}
	\item One file per cell, with the condition and replication in its name.
	\item Resume by skipping files that exist.
	\item Write to a temporary name and rename, so a killed job leaves no half file.
	\item Save \texttt{.Rds} files with \texttt{xz}; every file stays under 50 MB,
	and only summaries go to Overleaf.
	\item \texttt{tryCatch()} prints the task and replication of a cell to check,
	so that \texttt{monitor.sh} can list them.
\end{itemize}

\section{SLURM}

The default is a job array of blocks, with the replications in series inside each block.
SLURM does the managing; there is no GNU Parallel and no whole node.
This starts in free cores, loads R and the packages once per block,
and loses one block when a node fails.
It is not one array element per cell:
clusters limit the jobs a user can submit and the size of an array.

\subsection{Measure, Then Size}

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Measure: run a few cells of the slowest condition,
	and read \texttt{seff} and \texttt{sacct} for time and memory.
	\item Size: choose replications per block so that a block runs about one to four hours,
	within the job limit.
	Write the measured numbers in \texttt{.sim/README.md}.
	\item Check after the run: read \texttt{seff} on some elements, and update the numbers.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Blocks}

An element's index gives its task and range of replications by a fixed formula,
kept in \texttt{R}, so a block is reproducible and can be rerun alone.

\begin{itemize}
	\item Run the cells in a loop in one R process,
	with the seed and attempt rules above for each cell.
	\item Wrap each cell in \texttt{tryCatch()}, clear large objects between cells,
	and call \texttt{gc()}.
	\item Crashes: \texttt{tryCatch()} does not survive a segfault or an out-of-memory kill.
	Before a cell starts, write a marker with its task, replication, and attempt,
	and remove it when the cell ends.
	A rerun that finds a marker counts that attempt as failed and moves on,
	so a block does not hit the same crash forever.
	\item Rerun only the missing blocks, with a list in \texttt{sbatch --array}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Settings}

\begin{itemize}
	\item One core per element, and the measured peak memory plus a margin.
	\item Time: the measured block time plus a margin, not the partition's maximum.
	A shorter limit can start sooner,
	and a block that hits its limit loses only unfinished cells.
	\item Cap the elements running at once with \texttt{\%N} in \texttt{--array},
	starting low on a new simulation.
	\item Mail: \texttt{--mail-type=END,FAIL}, not \texttt{ALL}.
	\item Output and error files per element.
	\item Keep temporary files on the node, and compress when a stage ends.
	Summaries and compression also go through \texttt{sbatch}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{When to Use GNU Parallel}

Use a whole node with GNU Parallel only when a cell crashes often,
so that one process per cell pays for itself,
or when a job needs a node's memory.
Before moving a real simulation to blocks,
run one condition both ways and compare core-hours and time to finish.

\section{Running}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Before a full run: one or two replications inside the image,
	and \texttt{sbatch --test-only}.
	\item Agents never submit the full run: they give the user the command,
	with the measured time and memory.
	\item Results reach the paper through \texttt{Fig*()} and \texttt{.Tabular()}.
\end{itemize}

